  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard to check how it renders. The rule has been created but hadn't run yet when I last saw it (status \"pending\").\n\n**Data:** The logs have a `timestamp` field, `response` (a string, with a `.keyword` subfield), `extension`, `bytes`, `clientip`, `geo.*`, `machine.os`, `agent` and `request`/`url`. Response codes in the data are 200 (12,832 requests), 404 (801) and 503 (441).\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-abaf7beb Web logs overview\" has four panels:\n- **Total requests:** a single-number count.\n- **Requests over time:** a line chart on `timestamp`.\n- **Requests by response code:** a pie chart on `response.keyword`.\n- **Average bytes by extension:** a bar chart on `extension.keyword`.\n\nIt's at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-abaf7beb-web-logs-overview-7ffb434e75e4\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-abaf7beb 5xx spike\" (id `df135154-b068-4ec6-860e-031521f0e3d2`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- **Schedule:** it runs every minute.\n- **Condition:** it fires when more than 10 documents have a `response.keyword` matching `5xx` within the last 5 minutes.\n- **Actions:** it has no connectors attached, so it will show alerts in Kibana but won't notify anyone.\n\nThe sample data's timestamps may not fall inside a live 5-minute window, so the rule may not fire unless new data arrives.",
